Provide participating banks with secure access to the Paladin and Validium test environments through a centralized Identity and Access Management (IAM) layer.
This IAM layer ensures minimum viable security for authentication (AuthN) and authorization (AuthZ) by integrating an Identity Provider (IdP) service — Keycloak — and enabling Single Sign-On (SSO) across all services.

The goal is to establish a standardized, test-ready access control framework that simulates production-grade security principles while maintaining operational simplicity during integration and evaluation phases.

⸻

Scope
	•	Applies to all participants (banks) accessing Paladin and Validium environments.
	•	Covers user identity lifecycle, authentication, and authorization across all APIs, dashboards, and backend services.
	•	Ensures each participant has isolated identity domains (realms) with centralized policies.

⸻

Architecture Overview
	1.	Identity Provider (IdP)
	•	Keycloak acts as the central IdP, managing identities for all participant organizations.
	•	Each bank has a dedicated realm or organizational group for identity isolation.
	•	Supports federated login if the participant prefers to use its own corporate IdP (via SAML/OIDC).
	2.	Authentication (AuthN)
	•	Users authenticate through Keycloak’s SSO portal.
	•	Supports password, MFA, and token-based authentication.
	•	Session tokens (JWT/OAuth2) are issued and validated by backend services.
	3.	Authorization (AuthZ)
	•	Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) defines user privileges (e.g., Admin, Auditor, Operator).
	•	Access tokens include roles and organization claims for service-level enforcement.
	•	Backend microservices validate the JWT using the Keycloak JWKS endpoint.
	4.	SSO Integration
	•	All web and API services (Paladin, Validium, and auxiliary dashboards) integrate with Keycloak for SSO.
	•	Once authenticated, the user can access all authorized systems without re-login.
